      "result": "All 13 checklist items have been tested and recorded — **12 PASS, 1 FAIL**.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe application is largely solid. Listing, filtering/sorting, state persistence, detail pages, and the not-found handling all behaved as specified.\n\n**The one failure — IX-12 (panorama 360° rotation):**\n\nThe panorama rotates, but its yaw is **clamped rather than wrapped**. In a single continuous drag gesture sweeping 40,000px, `translateX` saturated at 973.95px and the rendered hotspot count dropped from 4 to **0 of 8**, never recovering at any sample point. Dragging back within that gesture stayed pinned at the same value with zero hotspots, leaving the user stranded in an empty view. Separately, repeated same-direction drag gestures were inert — 20 identical drags left the view frozen at `translateX: 75` — suggesting the drag origin isn't re-committed between gestures. Zoom itself was clean (clamps 0.5–2, all 8 hotspots visible at 0.5, fully recoverable), so the defect is isolated to non-wrapping rotation.\n\n**Two notes on methodology:**\n\n- **Hotspot clicks and drags needed a workaround.** Hotspot positions are recalculated every frame (as the spec requires), so Playwright's actionability check reported \"element is not stable\" and timed out. I dispatched clicks directly and, for drags, injected two inert marker elements *inside the `panorama.hotspots` layer* to give the drag precise, stable endpoints. My first drag attempt silently failed because I placed markers on `panorama.viewer` — the parent — while the drag handler lives on the inner `cursor-grab` layer; events never reached it. Worth knowing the failure was a harness artifact, not the app. I confirmed the final IX-12 verdict with real Playwright drags, not just synthetic events.\n- **CT-11 carries a caveat.** Image *relevance* is fundamentally a visual property, and the DOM-only mandate rules out screenshots. I verified what DOM evidence supports: each item has a distinct 3-image set with alt text matching its title, and no item renders another item's photo. I did not verify that the photos actually depict their subjects. The one cross-item shared asset (`photo-1513519245088`, used by both 春节 and 苏绣) is the intentionally broken URL that never renders — both locations correctly show the \"图片加载失败\" prompt, so no mismatched image is ever presented.",
